    "question": {
      ":question/id": "828cd382-e26d-5986-bae3-9f510a8f39a9",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186175910/images/parallel-35ace92d201d19bd71ec3a817762785207a5d3ea7d8507f03bbd096f625638b7.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = -x + 5$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -x + 5\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -x + 5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-1\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-1$ that passes through the point $(0,3)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (3) &= -1(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= -x + (3) \\\\\ny &= -x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
