    "question": {
      ":question/id": "126edf55-54fb-5122-b14c-923ccea5da62",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186175910/images/parallel-6b8b6b0bb705e2da435e6679fad331d1603e19a5ccac000b7e844aceca0bca99.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y =-\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$ that passes through the point $(-2,-3)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-3) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x - (-2)) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{4}{3} + (-3) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{13}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
